"Print Book Options: Trim Sizes | IngramSpark (https://www.ingramspark.com/plan-your-book/print/trim-sizes)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ingramspark.com/plan-your-book/print/trim-sizes\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 121\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: \nL2: This is a search field with an auto-suggest feature attached. [Input: Search] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4:   * There are no suggestions because the search field is empty.\nL5: \nL6: # Print Book Options\nL7: \nL8: ### Flexibility to professionally publish your book the way you want, with the quality you need\nL9: \nL10: cite1†Image: Collage8_Paperback_Romance L11: \nL12: ## Outside the Book\nL13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: cite2†Image: image of your book | cite3†Image: mockup 2 L17: \nL18: ### Book Cover Finish\nL19: \nL20:  |\nL21: ### Book Binding\nL22: \nL23: Matte Cover: Soft, velvety feel, no shine, polished  |   Perfect Bound: Pages and laminated cover glued together at the spine\nL24: Gloss Cover: High shine, smooth finish and texture  |\nL25: \nL26: cite4†Image: your book laying flat | cite5†Image: image of your book | cite6†Image: your book laying flat L27: \nL28: ### Book Cover Options\nL29: \nL30:  |\nL31: \nL32: ### Book Cover Finish\nL33: \nL34:  |\nL35: ### Book Binding\nL36: Available with or without a dust jacket  | Matte Cover: Soft, velvety feel, no shine, polished  | Case Laminate: Laminated cover pasted on cover boards and folded over the edges\nL37:    | Gloss Cover: High shine, smooth finish and texture  | Jacketed Case Laminate: Case laminate cover that includes a laminated jacket folded over the cover\nL38:    | Digital Cloth Cover: Subtle, cloth-like look (available with or without dust jacket). Textured feel available only for books printed in the US and UK  |\nL39: ## Inside the Book\nL40: \nL41: * * *\nL42: \nL43: cite7†Image: open cookbook showing a recipe L44: ### Interior Color\nL45: \nL46: Black & White: Most economical, printed on crème or white paper using inkjet technology. Ideal for text-heavy books.\nL47: \nL48: Standard Color: Standard color quality, printed on white paper using inkjet technology. Ideal for books that have some color.\nL49: \nL50: Premium Color: Higher color quality with vivid color and crisp edges, printed on white paper using inkjet technology. Ideal for image-heavy books and children’s books.\nL51: Ultra-Premium Color: Best color quality with ink laser-printed on the page, leaving a satin finish with light sheen and vibrant colors. Ideal for photography books, coffee table books, and high-quality children’s books.\nL52: ### Paper Color and Weights\nL53: \nL54: Groundwood 38lb / 56gsm: Available for black & white printing, and select, small-format trim sizes\nL55: \nL56: \nL57: \nL58: Crème 50lb / 74gsm: Available for black & white printing\nL59: \nL60: \nL61: \nL62: White 50lb / 74gsm: Available for black & white printing and standard color printing\nL63: \nL64: \nL65: \nL66: White 70lb / 104gsm: Available for all color printing options\nL67: \nL68: \nL69: \nL70: *Ink and paper options may not be available at all book trim sizes. Not all Ink options will be available for all paper options.\nL71: cite8†Image: birds-eye view of a book L72: ## Need Help Picking a Trim Size for Your Book?\nL73: \nL74: ## Book Trim Size Options\nL75: \nL76: * * *\nL77: \nL78:   cite9†Image: custom book dimensions | cite10†Image: custom book dimensions L79: \nL80: ### White Paper\nL81: \nL82: Paperback books with white paper can be sized between 4 inches wide x 6 inches tall (152mm x 102mm) and 8.5 inches wide x 11 inches tall (280mm x 216mm).  |\nL83: ### Creme & Groundwood Paper\nL84: \nL85: Paperback books with crème or groundwood paper can be sized between 4 inches wide x 6 inches tall (152mm x 102mm) and 6.14 inches wide x 9.252 inches tall (235mm x 156mm).\nL86: \nL87: \nL88: \nL89: \nL90: \nL91: cite11†Image: custom book dimensions | cite12†Image: custom book dimensions L92: \nL93: ### White Paper\nL94: \nL95: Hardcover books with white paper can be sized between 5 inches wide x 8 inches tall (203mm x 127mm) and 8.5 inches wide x 11 inches tall (280mm x 216mm).  |\nL96: ### Creme & Groundwood Paper\nL97: \nL98: Hardcover books with crème or groundwood paper can be sized between 5 inches wide x 8 inches tall (203mm x 127mm) and 6.14 inches wide x 9.21 inches tall (234mm x 156mm).\nL99: ## How Much Does It Cost to Print a Book?\nL100: \nL101: * * *\nL102: \nL103: The cost to print a book depends on the page count of your book and the specifications you choose.\nL104: \nL105: \nL106: \nL107: For example, hardcover books cost more to produce than paperbacks; color printing costs more than black & white; books with more pages cost more than those with less.\nL108: \nL109: \nL110: \nL111: Use our printing cost calculator to determine the cost of your book.\nL112: ### JOIN OUR MAILING LIST\nL113: \nL114: By clicking the \"submit\" button, you are agreeing to receive future marketing e-mail messages from IngramSpark.\nL115: \nL116: ### Quick Links\nL117: \nL118: © Copyright Lightning Source LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nL119: \nL120: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com cite13†Image†www.facebook.com --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGet Help | Cornell Print Services (https://printservices.cornell.edu/get-help)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://printservices.cornell.edu/get-help\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 114\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2: [Input]\nL3: \nL4:   * cite1†Home L5:   * cite2†About Us L6:     * cite3†Our Staff L7:     * cite4†Reviews L8:   * cite5†Services L9:   * cite6†Products & Pricing L10:   * cite7†Place an Order L11:   * cite8†Get Help L12:     * cite9†Contact Us L13:     * cite10†FAQ L14:     * cite11†Students & Staff L15: \nL16: # Get Help\nL17: \nL18: cite12†Image: Get Help with your questions here L19: ## Help and How To\nL20: \nL21: Not sure where to start with your project? Confused about what type of paper or binding to choose? Read our helpful tips about paper types, binding types, terms used in printing, and other information you might need to know!\nL22: ### General\nL23:   * Quantity: This refers to the total number of finished pieces required.\nL24: \nL25:   * Number of Pages: This refers to the total number of individual pages to be printed. For example, a standard postcard is printed on both the front and back. In this case the total number of pages would be 2.\nL26:   * *Number of Pages for booklets: A common mistake when requesting booklet printing is to list the number of spreads. A spread is simply a set of pages (usually two) viewed together. In order to provide accurate pricing, we need to know the number of individual pages. Common page sizes include 5.5” x 8.5”, 6” x 9”, and 8.5” x 11”.\nL27: \nL28:   * Please Note: Saddle stitch documents need to be built in page multiples of 4 pages (8, 12, 16, etc.) because they are printed on sheets that are folded in half.\nL29:   * Flat Size: This refers to the dimensions of a document after it has been printed and received any necessary trimming, but before it has been subject to any operations that further affect its size, such as folding. Flat size is often used interchangeably with Trim Size. For documents that are not folded, the Flat Size is also the same as the Finished Size.\nL30:   * Folded Size: This refers to the dimensions of a document after it has been folded. For example, a standard tri-fold brochure has a flat size of 8 1/2 x 11 with a folded size of 3.667 x 8.5.\nL31:   * File Submission: We accept print ready PDF files as artwork. On occasion, we will ask for a packaged InDesign file. Please make sure that anything that touches the edge of the page extends beyond the page 1/8 of an inch (bleed) so we have room to trim without having a white border. For more information about bleeds, please visit here: cite13†Adobe InDesign†helpx.adobe.com PDFs should be sent as single pages and NOT spreads. Please contact us if you need guidance in preparing your file for print.\nL32: * * *\nL33: ### Paper\nL34: \nL35: Different types and thicknesses of paper are usually used for different types of products. Read on to learn about the differences between types of paper we offer and what products they are best for.\nL36: Cover VS Text Weight Papers: A cover weight paper is going to be thick, similar in feel to card stock. Text weight papers are thinner, more like catalog and magazine paper. A paper labeled “cover” is always going to be thicker than a “text” paper. For example, 100# Gloss Cover is going to be much thicker than 100# Gloss Text.\nL37:   * 20/50# Text (non-glossy): Standard “copier paper”. This is an economical paper most commonly used for black and white, low-cost printing. This is relatively thin and may have show through with heavy coverage.\nL38: \nL39:   * 60# Text (non-glossy): This paper is slightly thicker than 20/50# “copy paper” An economical paper most commonly used for color, low-cost printing. This is relatively thin and may have show-through with heavy coverage.\nL40:   * 28/70#, 32/80# & 40/100# Text (non-glossy): These range from premium to ultra-premium stocks from left to right. Feels thick and substantial in your hands and is the best type of uncoated paper stock available for full-color printing. Show through is typically not an issue with heavy coverage. This would be a good choice for standard to higher end pieces like annual reports, newsletters or portfolio projects.\nL41:   * 80# Gloss Text (glossy, coated surface): Standard glossy paper provides an excellent opaque base for rich full color printing. This is a good choice for vibrant colors and photos. This is a standard paper for brochure printing, magazines, flyers, annual reports, etc.\nL42:   * 100# Gloss Text (glossy, coated surface): Similar to the 80# gloss text, but 25% thicker and heavier, for a more substantial feeling piece. This is a good choice for vibrant colors and photos. This is a good choice for higher end pieces like brochure printing, magazines, flyers, annual reports, posters, etc.\nL43:   * 80# Uncoated Cover (non-glossy): This paper is the thickness of a lightweight business card. This bright white, uncoated smooth cover stock. You can easily write on this stock. This is suitable for low-cost book covers, posters, business cards, etc.\nL44:   * 100# Uncoated Cover (non-glossy): This paper is the thickness of a standard business card. Bright white, uncoated smooth #1 grade cover stock is 14pt in thickness. You can easily write on this stock. This heavier option is a great companion to our 70# uncoated text for texture consistency across your marketing materials. This is a suitable option for appointment or reminder cards and business cards, book covers, etc.\nL45:   * 80# Gloss Cover (glossy, coated surface): This paper is the thickness of a postcard or baseball card. Coated with a glossy finish, making photographs and other images look beautiful. Standard uses: durable, heavy-weight brochures, catalog covers, and packaging.\nL46:   * 100# Gloss Cover (glossy, coated surface): This paper is the thickness of a standard business card. Coated with a glossy finish, making photographs and other images look beautiful. Not a good choice to write on. This a good choice for high quality magazine covers, business cards, postcards, fold-over greeting cards, etc.\nL47:   * 12pt Coated Two-Side Cover (glossy, coated surface): This paper is the thickest standard cover stock we offer. Coated with a glossy finish, making photographs and other images look beautiful. Not a good choice to write on. This a good choice for business cards, postcards, rack cards, bookmarks, etc.\nL48:   * 12pt Coated One-Side Cover (premium thickness, a glossy coated surface on one side & a non-glossy surface on the second side): This paper is the thickest standard cover stock we offer. Our most popular paper for postcards and greeting cards. This is a good choice for vibrant colors and photos on the coated side with the added benefit of being able to write on the second side.\nL49: * * *\nL50: ### Binding\nL51: \nL52: There are many different methods of binding documents together, so here is your go-to guide to learning about the different types of binding printed product. See below for illustrations of each.\nL53: cite14†Image: A normal looking book with pages bound together on the left with glue. Perfect Bind: Perfect binding refers to the method of binding where the cover and pages of the book are glued together at the spine and then the edges are “perfectly” trimmed to give your book its sharp, even edges. Perfect bound books are lightweight, flexible, and sleek.\nL54: cite15†Image: A book opened in the middle so you can see that the left and right pages are one page folded in half. You can see the staples down the center. Saddle Stitch: Saddle stitching refers to the method of binding where the pages are gathered together, folded and stapled along the crease, from the outside, to create a book. When setting up a booklet for saddle stitching you need your page count to be in multiples of four pages.\nL55: cite16†Image: A book with the pages bound together on the left with a spiral coil. Coil Binding: Coil binding is where a continuous plastic loop is inserted into punched holes along the spine. This binding allows books to lay flat when open.\nL56: cite17†Image: A book with the pages bound on the left by a long vertical piece of green tape. Tape Binding: Tape binding involves a strip of tape lined with a heat-activated adhesive or pressure sensitive adhesive. The tape is applied to the spine of a stack of paper. It’s affordable and stacks flat but is much less durable compared to perfect binding.\nL57: \nL58: \nL59: \nL60: * * *\nL61: ### File Preparation\nL62: \nL63: Please export your file to a High or Press Quality PDF\nL64: \nL65:   * How to from cite13†Adobe InDesign†helpx.adobe.com L66:   * How to from cite18†Microsoft Programs†office.microsoft.com L67: \nL68: * * *\nL69: \nL70: ### cite19†Contact Us L71: \nL72: ### cite20†Frequently Asked Questions L73: \nL74: ### cite11†Students and Staff L75: \nL76: \nL77: \nL78: cite21†Image: Cornell Print Services L79: ## Contact Us\nL80: \nL81: Cornell Print Services\nL82: 695 Dryden Road\nL83: Ithaca, New York 14850\nL84: (607) 255-2524\nL85: printservices@cornell.edu\nL86: \nL87: Store Hours:\nL88: Monday–Friday: 7:30am-4pm.\nL89: \nL90: Upcoming closings:\nL91: 7/2–7/3/2026 Holiday\nL92: 7/30/2026 Closed until 12pm\nL93: (open 12-4:15)\nL94: \nL95: Follow us on:\nL96: \nL97: \nL98: \nL99: ## Quick Links\nL100: \nL101:   * cite1†Home L102:   * cite2†About Us L103:   * cite5†Services L104:   * cite6†Products & Pricing L105:   * cite7†Place an Order L106:   * cite9†Contact Us L107:   * cite22†Equal Education & Employment†hr.cornell.edu L108:   * cite23†Web Accessibility Assistance L109: ## Web Accessibility\nL110: \nL111: If you have a disability and are having trouble accessing information on this website or need materials in an alternate format, contact cornellstore-web@cornell.edu for assistance.\nL112: \nL113: © COPYRIGHT 2025 Cornell Print Services | 695 Dryden Road, Ithaca, New York 14850\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhat is copyediting? (https://www.ciep.uk/resource/what-is-copyediting.html)\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ciep.uk/resource/what-is-copyediting.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 208\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: \nL2: cite1†Skip to content L3: \nL4: [Input: Search the site...]\nL5: \nL6:   * cite2†About the CIEP L7: \nL8: ### cite3†Our mission L9: \nL10: Learn more about the Chartered Institute of Editing and Proofreading (CIEP).\nL11: \nL12: cite3†Find out more L13: \nL14:   * cite4†Learn and develop L15: \nL16: ### cite5†Training courses L17: \nL18: Discover CIEP training courses.\nL19: \nL20: cite5†Find out more L21: \nL22:   * cite6†Membership L23: \nL24:     * cite7†Membership Upgrade Hub L25:       * cite8†Applying to upgrade your CIEP membership: A guide for members L26: ### cite9†Join L27: \nL28: All about CIEP membership.\nL29: \nL30: cite9†Find out more L31: \nL32: cite10†FAQs L33: \nL34: 11 Jan 2024\nL35: \nL36: # What is copyediting?\nL37: \nL38: Find out what to expect from a copyeditor: what they do, how they work, the kinds of edits they make, how long editing takes and the standards they adhere to.\nL39: ##### Copyediting's place in publishing\nL40: \nL41: ‘Copy’ is another word for text that is to be published, from a company report to a book to product packaging (or anything else with words).\nL42: \nL43: In the traditional publishing workflow, copyediting comes after developmental and stylistic line editing, and involves:\nL44: \nL45:   * agreeing final content\nL46:   * checking grammar, spelling and punctuation\nL47:   * applying consistency to the language and formatting\nL48:   * making sure it’s clear and reads well for its intended purpose.\nL49: Once a text has been copyedited, it’s ready for design and layout, and then proofreading.\nL50: ##### Who do copyeditors work with?\nL51: \nL52: Copyeditors work with readers, authors, publishers and designers/typesetters.\nL53: \nL54: #### cite11†Helping the reader L55: \nL56: A copyeditor helps the reader by, for example:\nL57: \nL58:   * making the structure of the document as clear as possible, including signposting elements like headings, notes, illustrations, references and glossary\nL59:   * making the language appropriate and understandable for the likely reader, with vocabulary choice, grammar and punctuation that bring clarity.\nL60: #### cite11†Helping the author L61: \nL62: A copyeditor helps the author by, for example:\nL63: \nL64:   * ensuring their message is clear and achieves its purpose\nL65:   * correcting or querying anything that seems to be an error, including misspelled names, misused words, numbers that don’t add up, incomplete references and inconsistent arguments\nL66:   * suggesting ways to reduce the length of a text, use the available space better or integrate new material most effectively.\nL67: #### cite11†Helping the publisher L68: \nL69: A copyeditor helps the publisher by, for example:\nL70: \nL71:   * getting the text into fit shape to go to the next stage of the publishing workflow\nL72:   * highlighting possible legal issues such as plagiarism or breach of copyright, libel, obscenity or actionable discrimination.\nL73: #### cite11†Helping the designer L74: \nL75: A copyeditor helps the designer or typesetter by, for example:\nL76: \nL77:   * providing a clean text document that can be formatted or imported into design software to prepare the final output\nL78:   * marking up the document structure and elements like special characters and illustrations so that the designer knows where they go.\nL79: ##### Learn how to copyedit\nL80: \nL81: Learn how to be a professional copyeditor with our suite of online training courses that take you from beginner to ready for market.\nL82: \nL83: cite12†Image: copyediting-1-course.png L84: \nL85: cite13†Find out more L86: \nL87: cite14†Image: copyediting-2-course.png L88: \nL89: cite15†Find out more L90: \nL91: cite16†Image: copyediting-3-course.png L92: \nL93: cite17†Find out more L94: #### cite11†Understanding house style L95: \nL96: Many changes at copyediting stage are made for consistency, either within the document or publication or to comply with a client’s style guide.\nL97: \nL98: Our short guide cite18†Your House Style†ciep.pixl8.cloud explains how to create a style guide.\nL99: #### cite11†What does copyediting involve? L100: \nL101: The tasks carried out during copyediting will vary depending on the nature of the text, how and where it will be published, what work has or will be carried out by someone else, and practical considerations such as the budget and time available.\nL102: \nL103: Here are some typical copyediting tasks.\nL104: #### cite11†Checking L105: \nL106: Checking that all elements of a text or document are present, in the right order and are referred to in the same way throughout. For example:\nL107:   * Checking that any references to content or features elsewhere in the text (cross-references) are accurate.\nL108:   * Checking that basic facts and arguments are plausible, consistent and reasonable.\nL109:   * Querying any language that is non-inclusive or problematic from a legal point of view.\nL110:   * In fiction, checking that characters’ physical characteristics and names stay the same, and that the timeline and the fictional world hold together, and keeping the narrative voice consistent.\nL111: #### cite11†Correcting and consistency checking L112: \nL113:   * Correcting errors or inconsistencies in spelling, punctuation, grammar, style and usage.\nL114:   * Checking and correcting spellings of names, for example of places or people.\nL115:   * Imposing consistency in use of, for example, italics, bold and capitals.\nL116:   * Improving clarity by rewording or reformatting text that is confusing or convoluted, or suggesting structural additions to help readability such as headings or lists.\nL117: #### cite11†Cleaning L118: \nL119:   * Cleaning up the document before it goes for design/layout (for example by removing unwanted formatting and extra spaces).\nL120:   * Marking up the structure of the document (such as heading levels, boxed items, lists, figures and images).\nL121: #### cite11†Communicating L122: \nL123:   * Creating and populating information documents (such as a style sheet and instructions for the designer/typesetter).\nL124:   * Liaising with the author or intermediary on anything that the copyeditor cannot confirm alone, such as preferred points of style, approval of suggested rewording or the location of missing information.\nL125: \nL126: \nL127: \nL128: #### cite11†How do copyeditors work? L129: ##### In Word\nL130: \nL131: Copyeditors usually mark up edits in a Word document using Track Changes. After an agreed process of feedback from the client and revision, they produce a final edited version of the text.\nL132: \nL133: ##### On hard copy\nL134: \nL135: Less commonly these days, the client may want the edits marked on a printout (hard copy), in which case someone other than the editor will need to make the corrections to the final document.\nL136: ##### In other software\nL137: \nL138: Sometimes copyeditors work directly on the layout itself, in software such as InDesign, PowerPoint, a content management system or a web editor.\nL139: \nL140: Once the copyeditor’s changes have been made and checked, the text should be ready to send to a designer or typesetter.\nL141: \nL142: ##### Who’s responsible?\nL143: \nL144: While a professional copyeditor will always aim for the highest standards, any remaining errors are ultimately the responsibility of the publisher.\nL145: #### cite11†Will a copyeditor make your text perfect? L146: \nL147: No professional copyeditor should promise to make your text perfect.\nL148: \nL149: No matter how well trained, experienced and diligent they are, they are still human.\nL150: \nL151: Furthermore, perfection is a subjective concept. While some errors are indisputable, others aren’t. At copyediting stage, many changes are made simply for consistency or based on style preferences.\nL152: #### cite11†Is there an acceptable rate of errors for copyediting? L153: \nL154: Even though copyeditors can’t make text perfect, it’s important that they work to a high standard.\nL155: \nL156: Some people will assert that copyeditors should catch a certain percentage of errors, but we don’t believe this is helpful because:\nL157: \nL158:   * some errors are subjective in nature\nL159:   * the copyeditor will be working within other constraints such as budget and schedule.\nL160: We recommend thinking in terms of whether the copyeditor has made the text fit for purpose within the limits of their brief. There should be consistency and clarity, and no barriers to the reader understanding the meaning of the text.\nL161: #### cite11†Upholding copyediting standards L162: \nL163: The CIEP is committed to upholding and promoting high editorial standards, which is why all our members formally agree to abide by our Professional Practice Code.\nL164: \nL165: We also have a formal procedure for complaints and appeals on the rare occasion we receive them.\nL166: \nL167: cite19†READ THE CIEP PROFESSIONAL PRACTICE CODE L168: \nL169: #### cite11†How long does copyediting take? L170: #### cite11†Complexity factors L171: \nL172: How complex, difficult or badly written the draft text is determines the pace at which a copyeditor can work.\nL173: \nL174: Unless the material is straightforward, the copyeditor will probably need to go through it several times to cover different elements such as structure, stylistic consistency, cross-references and meaning.\nL175: #### cite11†Efficiency factors L176: \nL177: A copyeditor cannot work for hours at a time and remain efficient. It also takes time to get up to speed, reading or rereading the brief and style guide, so short jobs are proportionately slower.\nL178: #### cite11†Estimated speeds L179: \nL180: With the caveat that each job is different and must be assessed on its own factors, an experienced professional may copyedit around 1,000 to 3,000 words per hour on a typically straightforward text.\nL181: \nL182: If the text needs heavy editing or rewriting, the rate may be much slower.\nL183: If the budget or schedule is too tight, the concept of ‘good enough’ may need to be adjusted accordingly and agreed with the client. In that case, the copyeditor will minimise and simplify any changes, or agree with the client which tasks to omit.\nL184: #### cite11†How should I brief a copyeditor? L185: \nL186: Make sure the copyeditor understands your needs, so they can meet your expectations.\nL187: \nL188: You can find guidance in our free booklet Proofreading or editing? A quick guide to using editorial professionals.\nL189: \nL190: cite20†READ THE FREE GUIDE L191: \nL192: cite21†Return to listing L193: \nL194: Suite 651\nL195: Milton Keynes Business Centre, Foxhunter Drive\nL196: Linford Wood, Milton Keynes\nL197: MK14 6GD\nL198: cite22†[email protected] L199: +44 (0) 1908 467770\nL200: Design & Development by cite23†Pixl8†pixl8.com L201: Membership software by cite24†ReadyMembership†readymembership.com L202: ###### Chartered Institute of Editing and Proofreading\nL203: \nL204: The Chartered Institute of Editing and Proofreading (CIEP) is a non-profit body promoting excellence in English language editing. We set and demonstrate editorial standards, and we are a community, training hub and support network for editorial professionals – the people who work to make text accurate, clear and fit for purpose.\nL205: \nL206: © 2026 Chartered Institute of Editing and Proofreading\nL207: Company number RC000906\n"
